      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "PURPOSE",
          "content": "To determine whether restenting for recurrent coronary-subclavian syndrome is technically feasible, provides durable results, and is a reasonable alternative to surgery."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE REPORT",
          "content": "A 58-year-old woman with a left internal mammary artery (LIMA) bypass to the left anterior descending artery underwent angioplasty and stent placement for left subclavian stenosis and coronary-subclavian steal. Twenty-three months later, she returned with progressive angina and left arm claudication; heart catheterization demonstrated restenosis of the subclavian artery at the stent site with recurrence of the coronary-subclavian steal. Successful redo angioplasty and stenting resulted in normal antegrade flow through the LIMA graft. The patient has remained asymptomatic for 3 years without evidence of recurrent in-stent stenosis on serial noninvasive studies."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Restenting is technically feasible and appears to be a durable response to subclavian in-stent restenosis in patients with coronary subclavian steal."
        }
      ]},
